  I risked clicking through to the form and going partway through it.

  Unfortunately there is no way that anyone associated with R is going
to fill out this form for you, and most of the questions are
unanswerable (or not in a way that will be satisfactory to your IT
security people) anyway: e.g.

> Please provide the URL of the vendor's security center with supporting documentation for PCI, SOC2 type II or ISO27001 certification reports, Information Security Policies, Procedures, Data Flow Diagram, most recent Application Code Review or Penetration Testing Reports, etc , or email these documents to itgrm at westinghouse.com

 does this vendor have a security disclosure program, do they have a
cyber-defense program, have they been audited ... etc..

  It's possible that you could get a third-party vendor such as Posit
to sell you a security-certified version of R (which would not be any
different but would come with the relevant guarantees, audits, etc.
from the third-party vendor).  (Note that R is **not** a Posit
product; they would be selling you security guarantees, not the
software itself.)

  Or you could use SAS.
https://www.sas.com/en_ca/trust-center/sas-trust-security.html

  cheers
   Ben Bolker

